@ 17.1.1 - Proto-Indo-European to Gheg Albanian
# citation: Pogostick Man, from Wikipedia contributors (2013), “Albanian language”. Wikipedia, the
# 			Free Encyclopedia. <http://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Albanian_language>
	d dʱ > ð / V_V
	d dʱ > ð / r_
	ḱ > s / _{u̯,u,i̯,i}
	ḱ > k / _R
	ḱ > {t͡s,t͡ʃ} (“[a]rchaic relic”)
	ḱ > θ
	kʷ > s / _”E?
	kʷ > c / _B?
	kʷ > k / else?
#	ɡ́(ʱ) > d / %_C:[+sibilant]
	ɡʷ(ʱ) > {ɡ,z}
	bʱ dʱ ɡ́(ʱ) > b d dh
	s > ɟ / #_
	s > ʃ / V{i̯,u̯,r,k}_V
	sd > θ / medial
	sḱ > h / medial
	sp > f / medial
	st > ʃt / medial
	s > θ / sometimes, involving “[d]issimilation with following vowel”
	s > h / V_V
	i̯ > ɟ / {a,e,i}_
	i̯ > j / _B
	i̯ > ∅ / E_
	i̯ > h / V_
	u̯ > v
	Vn > V:[+nasal] / _C?
	n > ɲ
	l r > { l, l:[+ long] } { r, r:[+ long] }
	{m̩,n̩} l̩ r̩ > e uj {ri,ir}
	# Loss of laryngeals, with the possible exception of h₄, if it existed; h₃ and h₄
	# 			seem to have possibly fronted a following back vowel
	e:[+ long] i:[+ long] o:[+ long] u:[+ long] > o i e {y,i}
	a e i o > {a,e} (j)e {e,i} a
	# Gheg seems to have maintained or innovated vowel length whereas Tosk has not
	ə > ∅ / _#
	c ɟ > t͡ʃ d͡ʒ (for most speakers)